  "content": "<p>How the stardust gets made.</p>\n    \n\n        <p>\n                                         By \n                                                                                                                                                    </p>\n                                    <p><a href=\"https://mashable.com/author/elisha-sauers\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Elisha Sauers</a></p>\n            \n            \n            \n                    <p><img src=\"https://helios-i.mashable.com/imagery/defaults/fallback-thumbnail.fill.size_1600x900.1.png\" alt=\"Mashable Image\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" width=\"1600\" height=\"900\" /></p>\n                        <p>Elisha Sauers</p>\n                        \n                    \n                \n            <p>\n                Elisha Sauers writes about space for Mashable, taking deep dives into <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/why-nasa-going-to-moon-artemis\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">NASA's moon and Mars missions</a>, chatting up <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/nasa-artemis-2-moon-commander-reid-wiseman\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">astronauts</a> and history-making <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/black-hole-milky-way-discovery-sagittarius-a\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">discoverers</a>, and jetting <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/solar-eclipse-flight-delta-2024\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">above the clouds</a>. Through 17 years of reporting, she's covered a variety of topics, including health, business, and government, with a penchant for public records requests. She previously worked for <em>The Virginian-Pilot</em> in Norfolk, Virginia, and <em>The Capital</em> in Annapolis, Maryland. Her work has earned numerous state awards, including the Virginia Press Association's top honor, </p><a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruwl6a8d2Z4\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Best in Show</u></a><p>, and </p><a href=\"https://featuresjournalism.org/contests-2/sfj-27th-annual-winners-by-category/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>national recognition</u></a><p> for narrative storytelling. For each year she has covered space, Sauers has won </p><a href=\"https://www.headlinerawards.org/2024-online/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>National Headliner Awards</u></a><p>, including first place for her <a href=\"https://mashable.com/series/sex-in-space\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Sex in Space</a> series. Send space tips and story ideas to </p><a href=\"https://mashable.com/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#60050c091308014e130115051213201a0906060d050409014e030f0d\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>[email protected]</u></a><p> or text 443-684-2489. Follow her on X at </p><a href=\"https://twitter.com/elishasauers\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>@elishasauers</u></a><p>.\n            </p>\n                            <p><a href=\"https://mashable.com/author/elisha-sauers\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Read Full Bio</a>\n                    </p>\n                                <p>\n                                                                                                                             on <time datetime=\"Wed, 25 Nov 2026 10:00:00 +0000\">November 25, 2026</time>\n                                    </p>\n        <p>\n                            <a href=\"https://www.facebook.com/sharer.php?u=https%3A%2F%2Fmashable.com%2Fscience%2Fjames-webb-space-telescope-cassiopeia-a-stardust\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">\n    \n    Share on Facebook\n</a>\n<a href=\"https://twitter.com/intent/tweet?url=https%3A%2F%2Fmashable.com%2Fscience%2Fjames-webb-space-telescope-cassiopeia-a-stardust&amp;text=Centuries-old+dust+hints+at+a+dying+star%27s+last-minute+chaos\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">\n    \n    Share on Twitter\n</a>\n<a href=\"https://share.flipboard.com/bookmarklet/popout?v=2&amp;url=https%3A%2F%2Fmashable.com%2Fscience%2Fjames-webb-space-telescope-cassiopeia-a-stardust&amp;title=Centuries-old+dust+hints+at+a+dying+star%27s+last-minute+chaos\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">\n    \n    Share on Flipboard\n</a>\n                    </p>\n    \n\n    \n\n            \n\n            \n                        <p><img src=\"https://helios-i.mashable.com/imagery/articles/05ZAexXyYsZOr0ajd5Bbhlm/hero-image.fill.size_1248x702.v1795553683.jpg\" alt=\"Webb analyzing Cas A's supernova remnant dust\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" width=\"1248\" height=\"702\" /></p><p>Dust that NASA's Webb telescope mapped in Cassiopeia A shows that the exploded star's inner layers mixed together before it blew apart.\n                \n                                    Credit: NASA / ESA / CSA / Tea Temim / Rick Arendt / Dan Milisavljevic / Joseph DePasquale \n                            </p>\n                                \n\n            \n            \n            \n            \n                            \n                                                            <p>The mix of elements that ends up in planets — even people — can hinge on a final shake-up inside <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/james-webb-space-telescope-planetary-nebula-star\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>a dying star</u></a>, new <a href=\"https://mashable.com/category/space\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>space</u></a> observations suggest. </p><p>A team of researchers studied <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/james-webb-space-telescope-cassiopeia-a\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Cassiopeia A</u></a>, a glowing cloud of gas and dust left behind by an exploding star. Using <a href=\"https://mashable.com/category/nasa\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>NASA</u></a>'s <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/webb-space-telescope-first-image-targets\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>James Webb Space Telescope</u></a>, the astronomers could parse out clumps within the 350-year-old cloud and analyze their compositions. The debris from the explosion, called a supernova, is still racing outward in space at about 11 million mph. </p><p>\"What's amazing is that the dust, which we are testing hundreds of years after the explosion, tells us about what happened to the star potentially only hours before it exploded,\" said Dan Milisavljevic, a Purdue University professor and co-author of the study, in <a href=\"https://www.purdue.edu/newsroom/2026/Q4/new-space-telescope-data-decodes-death-throes-of-stars/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>a statement</u></a>.</p>\n                    \n                <hr />\n                <p><strong>You May Also Like</strong></p><hr />\n            \n        \n        \n\n                    <hr />\n            \n<p><a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/james-webb-space-telescope-supernova-star-detected\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Supernova</u></a> explosions forge and scatter many of the heavy elements that later get recycled into new stars and worlds. Computer models have long predicted that a star's inner layers could merge before it explodes. But scientists don't fully understand why and how some stars go through this violent process. The <a href=\"https://iopscience.iop.org/article/10.3847/2041-8213/aea901#artAbst\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>new study</u></a>, published in <em>The Astrophysical Journal Letters</em>, adds evidence that it happened in Cassiopeia A, giving astronomers a way to peer inside a star that hasn't existed for centuries.</p>\n<p>A massive star builds up in layers, like an onion, with each shell fusing a different element. When the star explodes, some of that material cools into dust. Each layer makes its own kind of dust, so the grains, smaller than the width of a human hair, keep a record of what sat where inside the star.</p><p>As the layers merge, they can change the <a href=\"https://mashable.com/science/did-comets-bring-life-to-earth\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>mix of elements</u></a> the blast flings out, said Tea Temim, lead author of the study.</p>\n        <p>Mashable Light Speed\n        </p>\n        \n    \n<p>\"So, for example, the oxygen- and carbon-burning shells in a star merge together and change the structure of the star significantly,\" said Temim, a research scientist at Columbia University in New York. \"And that would then change the chemical abundances that you would see after a supernova explosion.\"</p>\n\n\n<p><em>Want more <a href=\"https://mashable.com/science\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>science</u></a></em><em> and tech news delivered straight to your inbox? Sign up for <a href=\"https://mashable.com/newsletters\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Mashable's Light Speed newsletter</u></a></em><em> or <a href=\"https://mashable.com/life/how-to-set-up-mashable-push-notifications\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>get Mashable push alerts</u></a></em><em> today.</em></p><p>To look for evidence, the team pointed Webb at three spots in Cassiopeia A, or Cas A, that NASA's retired Spitzer Space Telescope had previously flagged. Spitzer saw the debris as a blur, with light from many clumps mixed together. Webb's sharper view separates individual clumps, each roughly the size of our solar system, so the team could study them one at a time.</p><p>One clump held mostly silica dust, the main ingredient in most sand. Another held mostly carbon dust. The third sits in a feature nicknamed the <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/james-webb-space-telescope-cassiopeia-a\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Green Monster</u></a>, and its dust is rich in magnesium and silicon. That material didn't come from the blast at all; rather, the star shed it before it exploded.</p>\n<blockquote>\n    <a href=\"https://twitter.com/NASAWebb/status/2124881367297097971\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">\n        This Tweet is currently unavailable. It might be loading or has been removed.\n    </a>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>The researchers then compared their measurements with existing computer models of exploding stars. Only the models in which the star's layers had merged could reproduce both the silica-rich and the carbon-rich debris. A wider Webb dust map shows signs of the merger across much of the cloud.</p><p>The team cautions that those models are incomplete. Researchers will need more detailed 3D simulations — and Webb observations of more spots in the cloud — to confirm the merger. </p><p>This approach also could help astronomers make sense of supernovas in other galaxies.</p><p>\"I think it's remarkable that we can watch these changes and understand what they mean from thousands of light-years away,\" Milisavljevic said.</p>\n\n                    \n                                            \n                                    \n            \n            \n            \n                            \n            \n                    <p><img src=\"https://helios-i.mashable.com/imagery/defaults/fallback-thumbnail.fill.size_100x100.1.png\" alt=\"Mashable Image\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" width=\"100\" height=\"100\" /></p>\n                \n            <p>Elisha Sauers writes about space for Mashable, taking deep dives into <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/why-nasa-going-to-moon-artemis\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">NASA's moon and Mars missions</a>, chatting up <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/nasa-artemis-2-moon-commander-reid-wiseman\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">astronauts</a> and history-making <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/black-hole-milky-way-discovery-sagittarius-a\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">discoverers</a>, and jetting <a href=\"https://mashable.com/article/solar-eclipse-flight-delta-2024\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">above the clouds</a>. Through 17 years of reporting, she's covered a variety of topics, including health, business, and government, with a penchant for public records requests. She previously worked for <em>The Virginian-Pilot</em> in Norfolk, Virginia, and <em>The Capital</em> in Annapolis, Maryland. Her work has earned numerous state awards, including the Virginia Press Association's top honor, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ruwl6a8d2Z4\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>Best in Show</u></a>, and <a href=\"https://featuresjournalism.org/contests-2/sfj-27th-annual-winners-by-category/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>national recognition</u></a> for narrative storytelling. For each year she has covered space, Sauers has won <a href=\"https://www.headlinerawards.org/2024-online/\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>National Headliner Awards</u></a>, including first place for her <a href=\"https://mashable.com/series/sex-in-space\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\">Sex in Space</a> series. Send space tips and story ideas to <a href=\"https://mashable.com/cdn-cgi/l/email-protection#9afff6f3e9f2fbb4e9fbefffe8e9dae0f3fcfcf7fffef3fbb4f9f5f7\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>[email protected]</u></a> or text 443-684-2489. Follow her on X at <a href=\"https://twitter.com/elishasauers\" rel=\"noopener nofollow\" target=\"_blank\"><u>@elishasauers</u></a>.</p>\n        \n            \n            \n                            <p><img src=\"https://mashable.com/images/seamless-keep-scrolling.png\" alt=\"Mashable Potato\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" referrerpolicy=\"no-referrer\" />\n</p>\n                    \n\n                    \n            \n   